The rapid growth of telecom services has generated large-scale behavioral data, enabling predictive modeling for customer retention strategies. 
Customer churn prediction is a critical task for telecom operators, as retaining existing customers is significantly more cost-effective than acquiring new ones. 

This project investigates the application of machine learning techniques for predicting customer churn using a synthetically generated telecom dataset that simulates realistic usage patterns.

To simulate a realistic telecom environment, a synthetic dataset of 50,000 customers was generated. 
Customer attributes included contract information, usage behavior, payment patterns, and service interaction metrics. 

Churn probability was modeled using a logistic function incorporating behavioral risk factors such as complaint frequency, payment delay, contract duration, and usage characteristics. 
This approach ensures that churn is not randomly assigned but structurally dependent on customer behavior.

The churn rate was controlled by adjusting the bias term of the logistic risk function to simulate realistic customer behavior distributions observed in real-world telecom systems.
